# the argument of sidgwick-methods-1.md as claude-opus-5-5 read it: check that it says what the text says
# Sidgwick: ordinary moral reasoning mixes methods, yet systematic guidance requires knowing which judgments are ultimately valid.
# S4 is introduced with "Still", a contrast with S1-S3 rather than an inference from them, so C is given no derivation.
atom	phases_not_sharp	the three phases are not sharply distinguished in the moral reasoning of ordinary men
atom	intuit_not_sharp	Intuitionism of any sort is not sharply distinguished from either species of Hedonism (in ordinary moral reasoning)
atom	loose_common	a loose combination or confusion of methods is the most common type of actual moral reasoning
atom	believe_sense	most moral men believe their moral sense or instinct will guide them fairly right
atom	believe_rules	most moral men believe there are general rules for determining right action in different departments of conduct
atom	believe_explanation	most moral men believe a philosophical explanation can be found deducing these rules from fewer fundamental principles
atom	systematic	conduct is to be given systematic direction
atom	require_know_valid	we require to know on what judgments we are to rely as ultimately valid
# "It must not be thought that..." is read as a plain denial, not a modal claim.
P1	S1	phases_not_sharp ∧ intuit_not_sharp	kind empirical
P2	S2	loose_common	kind empirical
# S3 is hedged with "Probably"; the hedge is not modelled.
P3	S3	believe_sense ∧ believe_rules ∧ believe_explanation	kind empirical
C	S4	systematic → require_know_valid
